[38;5;231m⏺[39m This is rung 1 on the skill ladder — dropping a column destroys stored data — so I want a yes before writing it.

  [1mThe consequence:[0m [38;5;153mmigrations/003_drop_notes.sql[39m would run against production per the README's deploy job and
  permanently delete the [38;5;153mnotes[39m value for all 48,000 rows in [38;5;153musers[39m. [38;5;153mALTER TABLE ... DROP COLUMN[39m has no rollback; once the
  deploy job applies it, the only recovery is a database backup restore. "We stopped using it" tells me nothing reads
  the column now — it doesn't tell me the historical contents are disposable.

  Two things worth confirming before I write it:

  1. [1mIs the existing data disposable, or should it be preserved?[0m If there's any chance someone wants those notes later,
     the usual move is to dump them to an archive table or export first, then drop in a follow-up migration.
  2. [1mIs anything still writing to it?[0m A writer that outlives the drop fails at insert time, not deploy time. A grep of
     the app repo (not present here) would settle it — I only have the schema.

  If you confirm the data is disposable and no writers remain, here's exactly what I'll add — nothing fancy, just the
  drop, plus the matching [38;5;153mschema.sql[39m edit since the README says it's kept in sync by hand:

  [32m-- migration 003: drop notes[39m
  [34mALTER TABLE[39m users [34mDROP[39m [34mCOLUMN[39m notes;

  One caveat on the SQLite side: [38;5;153mDROP COLUMN[39m needs SQLite 3.35.0+ (2021-03). Older engines need the 12-step
  table-rebuild dance instead. Say the word and I'll write it.

